WEBVTT

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.470
- This is Moscone Center in San Francisco,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:44.270
home of political conventions, auto shows, and trade fairs.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:45.710
But for a few days this week,

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:48.050
it's the center of the Macintosh world.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.870
Today, we'll take you on a guided tour

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:52.570
of the 1989 Macworld Expo

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:58.930
on this edition of the Computer Chronicles.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:24.070
Chronicles is made possible in part by McGraw-Hill, publishers of Byte Magazine, VIX, the Byte

00:01:24.070 --> 00:01:30.000
Information Exchange. In print and online, Byte and VIX serve computer professionals worldwide

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.850
worldwide with detailed information on new hardware, software, and technologies.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.750
Welcome to the Computer Chronicles. I'm Stuart Chiffey, and with me this week here at the

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:46.870
Macworld Expo is Jerry Burrell, Editor-in-Chief of Macworld Magazine. Jerry, you've been on

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:50.490
the floor for a couple of days now. What are the most interesting new products or trends

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:51.440
you've seen?

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.420
Apple's announcement of its 030 had to be the most important thing here at the show.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.000
That allows us to make the marketplace grow, and it allows everybody else to develop new

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.670
new products. The four categories of things that I'd pick out of the entire show floor would be

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:10.110
the communications devices that we see, a lot of growth there, color hard copy output. That's very

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.490
exciting. It's been holding up the whole marketplace. Color and video devices allow us to go out into

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:19.350
the broadcast market from the Macintosh. And believe it or not, accelerator cards for the

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:24.530
Macintosh. If you had to name, say, two hardware products that really caught your attention here,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:30.000
what would they be? Apple's S-E-0-3-0. That sets the tone for the market. It gives

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.240
market opportunities for third-party developers both hardware and software and

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:38.400
the Ricoh erasable optical drives that are in a couple of boosts downstairs

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.360
that's a technology we saw on the next machine it's here it's shipping now it's

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.370
around $4,000 of the SCSI interface for Macintosh CPUs. Jerry today we're going

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:51.510
to take a look at that new Mac SE30 we'll see a new generation of desktop

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.420
publishing and presentation software including lots of products using color

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:00.000
and sound we'll see a new version of HyperCard called SuperCard and we'll take

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.600
a look at a new interactive CD-ROM game for the Macintosh, so stay with us.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:12.230
As they have grown, Macworld Expos have become flashier and glitzier, and San Francisco's

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.030
Macworld Expo 89 continued the trend.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.130
This year's Expo had all the elements of a major trade show, including music, magic,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.410
and massive crowds.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.280
The show was a launching pad for Apple's newest Macintosh called the SE30.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.000
The new machine uses the full 32-bit Motorola 68.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.900
88030 processor running at twice the clock speed of the first SE model. Apple claims

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:41.390
the new Mac can run at up to four times the speed of the original SE. The SE30 comes with

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:47.920
a 68882 math coprocessor, and it can be configured with up to 8 megabytes of RAM.

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.240
In a clear concession to the real-world mix of computer hardware, the SE30's drives can

00:03:53.240 --> 00:04:00.000
read and write to both Macintosh and IBM standards. Incremental improvements were also seen

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.360
in some traditional word processor packages.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.380
Claris showed off MacWrite 2 with new features intended to bridge the gap

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:11.160
between word processors and desktop publishing products.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.410
MacWrite 2 features multiple columns, multiple documents, and page preview.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.950
The program will import files directly from Microsoft word processors

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:25.890
and it offers direct insertion and editing of MacPaint and picked graphics.

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:29.820
MacRide 2 is available now and costs $249.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:37.070
Silicon Beach introduced its new version of HyperCard called SuperCard, a second-generation

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.260
program that is HyperCard compatible.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:45.340
SuperCard features colored drawing, painting and auto-trace, and a function called SuperEditor

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:50.380
allows users to reveal all the cards, menus, windows and resources of the application.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:57.310
It is very similar to HyperCard in that it uses the same metaphor of stacks and buttons

00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.540
and cards.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.840
It is a second-generation product, essentially.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.060
We've gone a lot further.

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.710
Cards of any size, color, scrolling windows,

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:10.410
all the things that people want in that kind of product now.

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.590
So we're going to give them a lot more capabilities, menus,

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.130
and it creates standalone applications.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:21.430
What that means is you can make a program look just like a Macintosh program

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.160
created with this authoring system.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.540
So another way of describing is an authoring system.

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:30.000
For non-HyperCard programmers, Texas.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.860
instruments demonstrated Action,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:33.980
a graphic interactive software

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.080
system to simplify developing

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:37.820
Mac-based user interfaces.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.700
Action comes with a user

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.620
interaction manager, a menu

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.700
management system, and an interactive

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.830
graphic editor. The program will

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.710
be available in the first quarter of this year

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:49.350
for around $2,000.

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.440
While the big hardware news here

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.200
is the introduction of the SE30,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.370
there's also a lot of attention being paid

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.310
to peripherals, things like erasable

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:00.000
optical drives, color printers,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.950
and accelerator boards.

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:08.050
Although the original Macintosh was and continues to be a black and white machine, that hasn't

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.550
stopped printer manufacturers from taking the next big step to color.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:17.990
Schlumberger Graphics unveiled a color postscript printer aimed at computer-aided design, engineering,

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:19.710
and manufacturing markets.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:25.570
The 5232 color postscript printer takes true color Adobe postscript images and prints at

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:30.000
300 dpi using color thermal transfer technology.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.200
can be configured for A-size or B-sized pages

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:36.100
and connects to standard AppleTalk, Centronics,

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.600
and serial interfaces.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.960
Prices start at around $17,000.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:44.410
Tektronix unveiled two color printers,

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.410
including a low-end machine for budget-sensitive buyers

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.710
called the ColorQuick inkjet printer.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:51.700
The ColorQuick is no speed demon,

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:54.100
making you wait two minutes for a single page,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:57.610
but on the other hand, it will retail for under $2,500,

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.000
a fraction of the cost of thermal transport.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:07.720
Tektronix also introduced the color PostScript compatible Phaser CPS for network users.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:12.580
The Phaser CPS has an AppleTalk network interface, a 40-megabyte hard disk,

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.400
and it mimics an ordinary laser writer to other devices on the AppleTalk network,

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:20.320
so pre-existing software need not be altered.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:27.300
The Phaser CPS sells for $16,000 and will be available in the first quarter of this year.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.000
Eastman Kodak had a printer of a different color,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.100
video printer that provides continuous tone color prints from video and digital signals.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:41.480
The printer has a built-in frame store and generates a copy from memory in about 90 seconds.

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:47.000
The unit will print up to 256 density levels for each of three colors at a resolution of

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:49.520
450 lines.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.440
To help bring artwork into the Macintosh, Thunderwear took the wraps off a handheld scanner called

00:07:54.440 --> 00:08:00.000
Lightning Scan. The portable scanner will scan images up to four inches wide and variable

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.020
resolutions of 100 to 400 DPI.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.640
Software comes with the scanner for image editing, contrast and brightness controls,

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.380
and real-time display and rotation.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:16.770
Lightning Scan is available now and retails for $549.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:21.590
The original SE got a boost from Accelerator card vendors, who eagerly joined the race

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:23.930
for faster and faster Macs.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:29.830
Dove Computer showed its latest in 68030 boards, called the Marathon 030SE.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.260
The board operates at 32 megahertz and the company claims a performance increase of up

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:40.010
to double that of a standard Mac SE. Dove also unveiled an O30 processor for the Macintosh

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:45.590
2 line called the Marathon O30X, offering twice the clock speed and an average performance

00:08:45.590 --> 00:08:55.750
improvement of 25%. The O30SE sells for about $2,000 while the O30X lists for $1,600.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:09:00.000
Cards were also on hand at the TPS electronics booth. TPS brought out its

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.800
smart card ADB, a solid-state memory card the size of a conventional credit card.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:11.300
The smart card encodes and reads up to 512 alphanumeric characters and can be re-encoded

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:12.620
up to 10,000 times.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.980
The company sees applications in the areas of medical records and patient ID, employee

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:19.920
badges, and debit card systems.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.980
The smart card system sells for $525.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.820
We'll have more from Macworld Expo 89 in just a moment.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.710
There was progress in storage devices at this year's show.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:52.080
Microtech introduced a $1,200 high-capacity removable cartridge disk drive for Mac Plus, SE, and the Mac 2 series.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:10:00.000
The Microtech R45 boasts over 42 megabytes of capacity and a competitive access time of 25 milliseconds.

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:06.020
The drive comes with dual SCSI ports for daisy chaining and a universal input power supply.

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:13.240
For users with really big storage needs, RICO introduced a 600-megabyte erasable optical drive.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:21.040
The $3,800 drive has a 50-millisecond average access time, and discs cost $250 apiece.

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.390
The drive will be available in late March.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:30.000
Software for optical media surfaced occasionally at the show, mostly for graphics and audio

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:34.000
Activision introduced the Manhole Adventure game on CD-ROM,

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.000
an interactive game developed from an original HyperCard version.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:41.920
The new CD-ROM game has 3D graphics, digitized voices,

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:43.920
and an original music soundtrack.

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.470
The Manhole is a point-and-click game.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.410
Clicking on an object takes players deeper into the game,

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:53.410
revealing greater and greater detail.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:56.410
The $50 game has over 600 graphic screens

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:59.410
and uses 55 megabytes of storage.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:03.130
The meager display of the original Macintosh

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:06.410
has led to a big market for add-on CRT screens,

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:08.910
but the Nexus company took the next step,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.010
introducing a series of gas plasma displays.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.110
The tarmac screens range in size

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:16.510
from 15 to 30 inches in diameter

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:19.760
and feature an integral touch panel in the front glass.

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.260
The amber displays are designed for CAD/CAM

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.000
and industrial applications

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:27.850
and are priced from $6,000 to $30,000.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:30.000
GreenSpring computers demonstrate

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.560
its confidence in the Mac's industrial appeal by introducing a rack-mounted

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:40.530
Macintosh 2 for real-time industrial control. The RackMac is packaged with a

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:45.150
specialized I/O controller board to provide for real-time I/O multitasking

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:50.250
and pipeline multiprocessing. According to the company, RackMac is adaptable to a

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:54.810
wide spectrum of industrial uses from controlling robots to operating commuter

00:11:54.810 --> 00:12:00.000
trains. Berkeley Systems caught the ear of

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.900
showgoers with the first Macintosh speech access package for the blind and

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:10.060
visually impaired. Outspoken is a mostly software package that transforms the Mac's

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:17.210
visual user interface into an audible one, accessible through the keyboard. The

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:22.350
program reads the screen of text-based displays and vocalizes by line, word, or

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:26.760
letter. Keyboard combinations take the place of mouse operations and the program

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.000
includes shortcuts for manipulating standard user interface.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.000
features. Outspoken sells for $395 and comes with a Braille quick reference card and embossed

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:55.010
representations of the Mac screen. The Macintosh has always been a graphics machine and graphics

00:12:55.010 --> 00:13:00.000
continue to drive the Mac, only now the Macintosh is more than just crisp pictures and desktop

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.280
publishing, it's becoming the machine for professional quality design and layout.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.000
The Mac's artistic potential has attracted the eye of the advertising industry both for

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:16.600
design and publishing. Multi-Ad Services introduced Multi-Ad Creator, a professional ad layout

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:21.790
program that brings together drawing and printing functions. The program is intended for layout

00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:28.080
and typographic control of single page documents in either black and white halftones or color.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.000
You can rotate text. You can

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:37.040
rotate graphics, you can fit text within an object, you can wrap it around an object.

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:43.700
There is a matrix command that allows you to create a particular coupon, for instance,

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:48.520
and you can determine the area that you want to fill, and it will automatically fill that

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:57.060
entire area from the cursor with those particular coupons and will retain the attributes of

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.000
the text that you have placed in them.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.880
desktop advertising publishers hope to repeat the success of traditional desktop publishing

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:09.320
by making ad agencies think twice before sending their copy out to the printer.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:14.760
Well, I feel there's a whole industry that really has been compromised. There's 25,000

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:19.840
ad agencies in the country, and there are over 10,000 newspapers, and there are about

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:26.680
100,000 retailers that put together ads locally every single day. And they're using products

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:30.000
right now that they really have had to compromise to do

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:34.950
what they specifically wanted to do in the battle to win over graphic designers

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:39.130
cricket software added color to its cricket paint program cricket color

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:43.370
paint incorporates many of the same tools as its monochrome cousin the

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:47.010
feature called fresh paint allows the user to create a shape and manipulate it

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:52.290
as an object or edit the shape as a bitmap color images objects shapes and

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.590
textures can all be saved as custom tools and the image can be viewed at two

00:14:56.590 --> 00:15:00.000
different levels of magnification simultaneously cricket

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.740
Color paint is due to ship in the first quarter of this year and will sell for around $300.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:14.410
For artists in the industrial and research worlds, several vendors showed advanced packages for computer-aided design and engineering.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:19.920
Schlumberger Technologies introduced McBravo, a high-end package for the Macintosh II.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:27.680
The Bravo software, originally designed for VAXs and special-purpose workstations, includes modeling and detailing programs.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.000
The modeler is a 3-D mechanical

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.560
design program that transforms two-dimensional designs into three dimensions with a drawing

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.210
tool called a work plane.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:43.890
Work planes are created separately and then combined to create a 3D object.

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:49.810
McBravo Modeler sells for about $1,500, and the drafting package called Detailer is available

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.990
for about $2,000.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:16:00.000
A company called 2AI claimed to have the first image processing software for the Mac 2, called

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.740
designed for scientists and physicians, BigMaj has extensive geometric, logical, and histogram

00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:11.960
functions. The program also features animation and magnified views and morphological operations

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:17.540
such as reconstructed images and skeleton views. BigMaj sells for $990.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:25.210
Making the leap from machine parts to designer labels, Modocad of Los Angeles introduced a CAD

00:16:25.210 --> 00:16:30.000
package for fashion designers. Modocad runs on the Mac 2 and includes more

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.300
modules for textile and fashion design, grading, and for pattern making.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:39.840
Fabric simulation tools allow the user to stretch a fabric and to add pleats or folds.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:45.120
A function called motodrape can simulate how a finished garment would look on a live model.

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:52.290
Crossfield of England filled its booth with some sophisticated publishing and page layout software,

00:16:52.730 --> 00:16:55.820
bringing the Macintosh one step closer to the professional printer.

00:16:56.620 --> 00:17:00.000
Crossfield demonstrated the light speed color layout system

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.690
for graphic design on the Macintosh 2.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:05.750
Lightspeed can incorporate photography, line art,

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:10.590
illustrations, headline type, logotypes, and text.

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:13.930
The program can crop, move, and resize scanned elements

00:17:13.930 --> 00:17:16.150
as they are integrated into a layout.

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:20.040
Pages can be any size up to 444 inches square.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.960
All elements, including type, can be rotated, skewed, stretched,

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.610
condensed, mirrored, and flipped.

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:30.000
For output, the user can choose from PostScript laser printer, color

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:33.060
thermal transfer, or go directly to an electronic color

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:35.320
prepress system.

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:38.120
One of the earliest names in desktop publishing history,

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:40.880
Adobe Systems made its regular appearance at a booth

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.720
manned by Adobe president and postscript inventor

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:47.640
John Warnock, who reflected on the sudden growth and maturity

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.670
of microcomputer publishing.

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.350
I think it's spreading out, and it's starting

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.270
to become sort of a part of the fabric of just the computer

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:56.720
business in general.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:59.640
And so it sort of started out as a niche

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.480
area and now it's sort of broadening its base into all aspects of visual communication

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.100
within corporations.

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:13.440
Adobe introduced an automated tracing program called Streamline for converting bitmap images

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:15.800
into Adobe Illustrator files.

00:18:15.800 --> 00:18:21.020
Once converted, images can be exported as is to other page layout programs or edited

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:24.800
with Adobe Illustrator software.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:30.000
Streamline will convert line drawings using either outline tracing, centerline tracing,

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:30.860
of both.

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:34.000
Streamline sells for $395.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:40.230
A lot of people can't draw, but they would like to use drawings in their publications.

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:44.550
And Streamline is a package that allows you to take a scanned black and white piece of

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:49.990
artwork, like a logo or something, and scan it in, and then Streamline will turn it into

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:52.290
an Illustrator file that you can then edit.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:55.420
So you don't have to be nearly as creative.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:19:00.000
Adobe's president also mused on desktop publishing's critical contribution

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:02.650
to the success of the Macintosh.

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.930
- It's a very highly integrated platform

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:07.510
and because it is and because it's so strong

00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:09.770
in the desktop publishing arena,

00:19:09.770 --> 00:19:13.200
it has a very strong niche in that portion of business.

00:19:13.200 --> 00:19:18.620
If you ask me if that still is very strategic to it,

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.120
I would say very definitely

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:23.860
because a lot of the Macintosh's credibility

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:26.590
is because of that aspect.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:28.700
- Altisys Corporation had a different way

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.000
of simplifying life

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.750
micro-publishers with a program called Keymaster. Keymaster creates high-resolution postscript

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:41.850
fonts from Macintosh artwork. Postscript quality drawings and logos can be dropped into documents

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:47.660
and manipulated like fonts. Keymaster imports images in EPS format from Aldous Freehand

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:53.630
and Adobe Illustrator and in PICT format from other object-oriented programs. Keymaster

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:59.060
was designed to address the problem of people having to continually cut and paste artwork

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:00.000
that they've got in existing.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.440
libraries into their documents.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:09.110
Now, instead of having to cut and paste, you can import your artwork, which you generated

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:15.610
in, say, FreeHands or Illustrator and saved in the encapsulated PostScript format, or

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:19.000
from any program that will generate a picked file format.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:25.630
I can now import your complex piece of artwork as a single character in a new font.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:29.730
This is a font that is a Macintosh font in the strictest sense of the word.

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:37.770
which once installed in the system is available from any application just like any other font.

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:54.650
Our coverage of Macworld Expo 89 continues in just a moment.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:21:00.000
Mass Microsystems presented its own picture show within a show at Macworld with a full-color

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.160
special effects video processor board.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.880
The ColorSpace FX card provides the Macintosh 2

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:08.640
with real-time video and image manipulation.

00:21:09.410 --> 00:21:12.210
ColorSpace FX can squeeze and shrink a picture,

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:14.650
create mirror and kaleidoscope effects,

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.190
and zoom in and zoom out from the image.

00:21:19.140 --> 00:21:22.740
ColorSpace FX is compatible with NTSC, PAL,

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:25.040
and CCAM video in any combination.

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:27.990
The board converts the interlaced video signal

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.000
into a non-interlaced RGB signal

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.600
compatible with a Macintosh monitor.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.930
ColorSpace FX is priced at under $3,000

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:38.950
and is due to ship in the second quarter of the year.

00:21:38.950 --> 00:21:42.100
Moving directly into the professional world

00:21:42.100 --> 00:21:43.120
of television production,

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:46.120
Julian Systems demonstrated EditWorks,

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:49.120
a sophisticated videotape editing and production system.

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:53.070
EditWorks is based around a rack-mounted Macintosh 2

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:56.070
that controls everything from time code calculation

00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.070
to edit list management and budgeting.

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:00.000
The mouse-based system,

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.560
takes the job of selecting video pictures on a video screen

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:08.460
and drops it into the picture-based interface of a Macintosh screen.

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:13.030
For Mac users more concerned with computer networks than television networks,

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:17.190
Dana Communications introduced an entry-level local area network

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.530
marrying Macs with IBMs.

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:23.190
The DanaNet consists of a server-based network operating system

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.350
and interface card for the server,

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:28.150
working over local talk or Ethernet cabling.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.880
Dananet is a special version of Novell Netware, customized for the Macintosh.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:41.500
Dananet can connect up to 100 Macs and/or IBM PCs, and comes bundled with Danamail,

00:22:41.500 --> 00:22:44.570
a Mac to MS-DOS electronic mail package.

00:22:44.570 --> 00:22:50.910
Dananet will be available in April, and prices start around $1,200.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:56.720
Finally, for users more interested in transporting data than sending it, Ex-Machina of New York

00:22:56.720 --> 00:23:00.000
unveiled its WristMac personal information system.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:05.240
The wrist Mac is a digital watch that can download files from your Macintosh through

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:08.930
a HyperCard-based software package.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:15.010
Each file can contain up to 79 entries, consisting each of two 12-character lines, for a total

00:23:15.010 --> 00:23:16.990
of 80 entries in the watch.

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:22.270
Typical files contain messages which can be timed to alarms, schedules, or phone numbers.

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:27.860
The watch connects to the computer through a cable that attaches to either of the Mac's

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:30.000
serial ports.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:34.460
a HyperCard stack is included that simulates the watch's face on the Mac monitor.

00:23:34.940 --> 00:23:37.260
Wrist Mac retails for $225.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:41.720
And that is Macworld Expo 89.

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:45.140
I'm Stuart Chiffey for the Computer Chronicles.

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:04.930
You've been hearing for years that you almost have a mainframe in that new super-duper micro on your desk.

00:24:04.930 --> 00:24:07.590
Well, you now can have a mainframe in your desktop.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:11.430
Opus Systems has announced its new personal mainframe series 400.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:16.380
It's a board containing the Motorola 88,000 wrist chip with 4 megabytes of RAM.

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:19.040
That's enough to run Unix on your standalone micro.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:22.880
Unix comes bundled with the board. The cost is around $5,000.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:27.600
Sanyo, Data General and Everex are also announcing standalone 88,000 PCs.

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:30.000
The price? They expect it to be around $10,000.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:39.000
Microsoft nudged closer to the UNIX world last week, buying a 20% interest in the Santa Cruz operation, a major UNIX software house.

00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:47.000
A recent market research study predicts a 29% annual growth rate for UNIX systems, compared to a 12% growth rate for all other systems.

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:52.000
News for speed freaks: Intel has revealed details of its new N10 Superchip.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:59.890
It's a risk-based chip that operates at 50 MHz. It features some parallel processing and a 64-bit data bug.

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:04.580
and Hitachi announced what it called the world's fastest 32-bit CPU.

00:25:04.580 --> 00:25:08.780
The company says the chip can do 70 million calculations per second,

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:11.290
twice as fast as its previous chip.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.590
Well, three Mac expos have come and gone since its announcement,

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:18.550
but InfoMix has now finally released its much-tatted Wings program.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:23.100
It's described as a graphic spreadsheet combining elements of HyperCard and Excel.

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:25.900
It's priced at $399.

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:30.000
Borland has released a new special student edition of its Quattro spreadsheet program,

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.200
and McGraw-Hill has announced that it will bundle the new quattro with selected college textbooks.

00:25:35.200 --> 00:25:37.760
The news was not so good for Leading Edge last week.

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:41.200
After announcing plans to sell the company to PC systems,

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:46.800
Leading Edge filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy, found itself under investigation by the FBI,

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:51.200
and ended up as a defendant in a lawsuit filed by six computer dealers,

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:54.360
claiming they had paid for computers they never received.

00:25:54.360 --> 00:26:00.000
Also bad news at the source, the online service says it is laying off one-fourth of its staff,

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:06.360
SOAR subscribers have plummeted to 50,000 after hitting a high of 80,000 two years ago.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:10.780
If you have an hour to kill at O'Hare in Chicago, you can now spend the time online.

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:15.410
The airport is installing new data access payphones with standard female phone jacks,

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:17.750
so you can plug the old laptop right in.

00:26:17.750 --> 00:26:21.890
Finally, there may be a computer in the audience at this year's Academy Awards ceremony.

00:26:21.890 --> 00:26:25.090
One of the nominees for Best Animated Short is "Ten Toy,"

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:30.000
an animated film created completely by computer at Pixar in San Rafael, California.

00:26:30.440 --> 00:26:33.080
That's it for this week's Random Access, I'm Cynthia Steele.

